t":"only","type":"word","start":60.64,"end":60.88},{"text":"the","type":"word","start":60.96,"end":61.04},{"text":"denominators.","type":"word","start":61.16,"end":62.32},{"text":"Re-cut","type":"word","start":62.4,"end":62.88},{"text":"first,","type":"word","start":62.96,"end":63.48},{"text":"so","type":"word","start":63.52,"end":63.6},{"text":"the","type":"word","start":63.68,"end":63.76},{"text":"pieces","type":"word","start":63.84,"end":64.16},{"text":"are","type":"word","start":64.24,"end":64.32},{"text":"the","type":"word","start":64.4,"end":64.56},{"text":"same","type":"word","start":64.72,"end":64.88},{"text":"size.","type":"word","start":64.96,"end":65.76},{"text":"Then","type":"word","start":65.84,"end":66.08},{"text":"compare.","type":"word","start":66.16,"end":66.88}],"text":"Here are four-fifths and seven-tenths. The bars are the same length, but the pieces are different sizes. So we can't just count yet. Let's re-cut. Cut each fifth into two pieces. Now both bars have tenths. Four-fifths is eight-tenths. Four times two is eight. Five times two is ten. Look, the shaded parts still line up. Re-cutting didn't change the amount. Eight-tenths is more than seven-tenths, so four-fifths is greater. That's Dion's way. He makes a common denominator. Emma has another way. Here are one-sixth and two-elevenths. She makes the numerators match. One-sixth is two-twelfths. Now both have two pieces. A twelfth is smaller than an eleventh. So two-elevenths is greater. That's a common numerator. Watch out! Don't compare only the numerators, or only the denominators. Re-cut first, so the pieces are the same size. Then compare."}}